# Let shorn (and so Claude's read-only checks) read the kernel log without a
# password. Kernel messages are where an Xid, a UVM assert or an OOM kill shows
# up first, and `kernel.dmesg_restrict=1` puts them behind root. Added
# 2026-10-19 for the UVM memory-saver trial (vllm-proxy docs/future-memsaver.md).
#
# READ-ONLY FORMS ONLY, ENUMERATED. A bare `/usr/bin/dmesg` here would allow
# any argument, and dmesg has writers: `-C`/`-c` clear the ring buffer (the
# evidence of the next fault), `-n`/`-D`/`-E` change console logging. In
# sudoers `""` means "no arguments", so the first entry is plain `dmesg`; the
# rest only change how the same buffer is printed. Filter with a pipe
# (`sudo dmesg -T | grep -i xid`), which needs no extra grant.
shorn ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/dmesg "", /usr/bin/dmesg -T, /usr/bin/dmesg --ctime, /usr/bin/dmesg -x, /usr/bin/dmesg -T -x
